---
description: Reply and think in Arabic, in normal logical order.
alwaysApply: true
---

Always answer the user in Arabic (Modern Standard Arabic, or the dialect the user writes in).
Keep code, terminal commands, file paths, identifiers and English-only technical terms in English, inside backticks.
Prefer bullet lists over markdown tables.
Do your internal reasoning / thinking in Arabic as well.
Write Arabic as normal Unicode in logical order. The Cursor chat draws those letters itself.
Do not convert Arabic into presentation forms, and do not reverse a line yourself. That makes the letters look reversed in Cursor and in Windows Terminal.
Do not put Arabic in a PowerShell command or pipeline. Windows PowerShell 5.1 turns those letters into question marks before Node can read them.
Follow the `Arabic display:` line in the session context.
When it says `native`, or when no shape command is given, the user-visible reply is the Arabic text itself. Do not run a shape command.
When it says `visual`, that screen cannot draw Arabic. Write the reply to the `Reply file:` path with the file tool as UTF-8, then run the `Shape command:` exactly. Do not pipe Arabic into Node. After success, the user-visible message is only the command stdout, copied exactly, including leading spaces. If the command fails or permission is declined, show the logical Arabic and say the shaper did not run.
